72740	72745	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ ALLAN ARMADALE ’S DREAM .
72746	72936	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ Early on the morning of June the first , eighteen hundred and fifty - one , I found myself ( through circumstances which it is not important to mention in this place ) left alone with a friend of mine -- a young man about my own age -- on board the French timber - ship named _ La Grace de Dieu _ , which ship then lay wrecked in the channel of the Sound between the main - land of the Isle of Man and the islet called the Calf . Having not been in bed the previous night , and feeling overcome by fatigue , I fell asleep on the deck of the vessel . I was in my usual good health at the time , and the morning was far enough advanced for the sun to have risen . Under these circumstances , and at that period of the day , I passed from sleeping to dreaming . As clearly as I can recollect it , after the lapse of a few hours , this was the succession of events presented to me by the dream :
72937	72973	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 1 . The first event of which I was conscious was the appearance of my father . He took me silently by the hand ; and we found ourselves in the cabin of a ship .
72974	72996	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 2 . Water rose slowly over us in the cabin ; and I and my father sank through the water together .
72997	73020	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 3 . An interval of oblivion followed ; and then the sense came to me of being left alone in the darkness .
73021	73026	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 4 . I waited .
73027	73077	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 5 . The darkness opened , and showed me the vision -- as in a picture -- of a broad , lonely pool , surrounded by open ground . Above the farther margin of the pool I saw the cloudless western sky , red with the light of sunset .
73078	73095	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 6 . On the near margin of the pool there stood the Shadow of a Woman .
73096	73145	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 7 . It was the shadow only . No indication was visible to me by which I could identify it , or compare it with any living creature . The long robe showed me that it was the shadow of a woman , and showed me nothing more .
73146	73167	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 8 . The darkness closed again -- remained with me for an interval -- and opened for the second time .
73168	73233	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 9 . I found myself in a room , standing before a long window . The only object of furniture or of ornament that I saw ( or that I can now remember having seen ) was a little statue placed near me . The window opened on a lawn and flower - garden ; and the rain was pattering heavily against the glass .
73234	73258	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 10 . I was not alone in the room . Standing opposite to me at the window was the Shadow of a Man .
73259	73313	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 11 . I saw no more of it ; I knew no more of it than I saw and knew of the shadow of the woman . But the shadow of the man moved . It stretched out its arm toward the statue ; and the statue fell in fragments on the floor .
73314	73355	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 12 . With a confused sensation in me , which was partly anger and partly distress , I stooped to look at the fragments . When I rose again , the Shadow had vanished , and I saw no more .
73356	73382	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 13 . The darkness opened for the third time , and showed me the Shadow of the Woman and the Shadow of the Man together .
73383	73404	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 14 . No surrounding scene ( or none that I can now call to mind ) was visible to me .
73405	73516	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 15 . The Man - Shadow was the nearest ; the Woman - Shadow stood back . From where she stood , there came a sound as of the pouring of a liquid softly . I saw her touch the shadow of the man with one hand , and with the other give him a glass . He took the glass , and gave it to me . In the moment when I put it to my lips , a deadly faintness mastered me from head to foot . When I came to my senses again , the Shadows had vanished , and the third vision was at an end .
73517	73533	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 16 . The darkness closed over me again ; and the interval of oblivion followed .
73534	73569	72722	72723	Mr. Hawbury	263	“ 17 . I was conscious of nothing more , till I felt the morning sun shine on my face , and heard my friend tell me that I had awakened from a dream .... ”
