ds . The only complaint I have to make is , I think , a just one . Mr. Armadale has been back at Thorpe Ambrose for some days . I have entreated him , by letter , to grant me an interview ; to tell me what dreadful suspicions he has of me , and to let me set myself right in his estimation . Would you believe it ? He has declined to see me -- under the influence of others , not of his own free will , I am sure ! Cruel , is n’t it ? But he has even used me more cruelly still ; he persists in suspecting me ; it is he who is having me watched . Oh , Mr. Midwinter , do n’t hate me for telling you what you _ must _ know ! The man you found persecuting me and frightening me to - night was only earning his money , after all , as Mr. Armadale ’s spy . ”
